option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODEL=Subsurface Scattering Model
value.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODEL.0=Legacy
value.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODEL.1=Rewrite
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODEL.comment=Legacy restores the pre-rewrite implementation (no brightness restriction beyond the shadow distance - screen-space shadows provide the darkening there). Rewrite uses per-material thickness, an ambient term, and is decoupled from screen-space shadows and the camera direction. The Front Diffusion / Backlight Translucency / Ambient / Radius options below only apply to the rewrite model.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODE=Subsurface Scattering Mode
value.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODE.0=Hardcoded Only
value.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODE.1=Hardcoded + PBR
value.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_MODE.2=PBR Only
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_STRENGTH=Subsurface Scattering Strength
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_BRIGHTNESS=Subsurface Scattering Brightness
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_FRONT_SUPPRESS=Front-Lit Suppression (old model only)
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_FRONT_SUPPRESS.comment=The old SSS formula contains no N.L and its thickness term is exactly 1 in PCF mode, so on a sunlit face it is simply extra light added on top of the diffuse: subsurface materials (wool, ice, leaves, grass) look brighter than normal blocks when directly lit. This option suppresses SSS by (1 - N.L): full when facing the sun, unchanged at grazing angles, so SSS becomes a terminator glow. 0 = vanilla behaviour (for A/B), 1 = fully suppressed (default).
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_EXTRA_GATES=Extra Gates (off = pure vanilla formula)
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_EXTRA_GATES.comment=The old SSS path is now a line by line port of the vanilla Revelation formula, and vanilla has no shadow or orientation gates at all: SSS follows the sun irradiance only, ignoring shadow visibility and face orientation. AD-edit added two on top - the shadow power gate (fades SSS with shadow visibility) and the back-face entry factor (fixes the abnormal glow when looking towards the sun beyond the shadow distance). Off disables both; combine with Front-Lit Suppression at 0 for 100% vanilla behaviour.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_CROSS_THICKNESS=Cross-Model Thickness (PCF only)
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_CROSS_THICKNESS.comment=Volumetric thickness applied to cross-model blocks only (materialID 1000-1003: grass, flowers, saplings, including the 1003 assigned by Unlabelled Foliage Detection). In PCF mode there is no blocker depth (sssBlockerDepth is always 0, so the thickness term exp2(0)=1) and SSS degenerates into a flat additive term; this gives it a thickness. 0 = back to the flat additive term (vanilla PCF behaviour), higher = darker and thicker looking. Has no effect in PCSS mode, where the real blocker depth is used.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_NONCROSS_THICKNESS=Non-Cross Thickness (PCF only)
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_NONCROSS_THICKNESS.comment=Same as above but for every other SSS material (leaves 13, vines 27/28, wool and hay 37, ice, snow and honeycomb 38, slime 51, ...). Default 0 keeps the vanilla flat additive behaviour in PCF; raise it if you want wool and ice to look thick too. PCF only.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_RADIUS=Subsurface Scattering Radius
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_RADIUS.comment=Diffusion mean free path (= value x 4 blocks). Higher = more translucent, weaker volumetric falloff. Also sets the blur radius of the screen-space diffusion step.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_TRANSMISSION=Backlight Translucency (view dependent)
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_TRANSMISSION.comment=Transmission glow for thin surfaces (leaves, grass, vines) when back-lit. This term contains the camera direction, so it only shows when looking towards the sun and pops as the camera turns - hence the default of 0 (off). Raise it if you want the backlit glow.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_DIFFUSE=Front Diffusion
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_DIFFUSE.comment=Brightness added on lit faces. Camera independent: it only follows N.L and shadow visibility, exactly like the old implementation (steady while turning the camera, responsive to lighting). At the default 0.35 a lit face is about 20% brighter than a normal block.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_AMBIENT=Ambient Subsurface
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_AMBIENT.comment=The only term that does not depend on the sun direction or the surface orientation, so it is what keeps SSS visible in shade, on back-facing surfaces and indoors (attenuated by AO and the indoor gate). 0 leaves only the two sun-driven terms.
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_DIFFUSION=Screen-Space Diffusion (currently disabled)
option.SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_DIFFUSION.comment=Blur the SSS source with a depth-aware half-res separable filter before compositing: real bleeding across edges and softer transitions over large areas. Off falls back to per-pixel SSS. Code and buffers are kept; re-enable by uncommenting #define SUBSURFACE_SCATTERING_DIFFUSION in settings.glsl. Disabled by default per request, so this toggle does not show up in the GUI right now.
option.SSS_DISABLE_BEYOND_SHADOW_DIST=Fully Disable SSS Beyond Shadow Distance
option.SSS_DISABLE_BEYOND_SHADOW_DIST.comment=Off by default: beyond the shadow distance SSS keeps running, but its sun-driven terms (front diffusion + backlight translucency) fade out as 1 - distanceFade, leaving only the sky/ambient term - so distant translucent blocks do not gain brightness from sunlight passing through and there is no hard cutoff. On restores the old behaviour of disabling SSS entirely outside the shadow distance.

